<Project>

  <!-- Imported at the END of each src/ project (unlike Directory.Build.props, imported at the
       START, before a project's own PropertyGroup runs), so $(OutputType) below already has its
       final value (e.g. Service.csproj's own RuntimeIdentifier-conditioned WinExe/Exe) by the time
       this Condition evaluates; a Directory.Build.props-based version of this same condition would
       silently never match, since OutputType isn't set yet at that import point. Scoped to Exe/
       WinExe outputs, so Base.csproj itself (a Library) is excluded; only Hello.csproj/
       Service.csproj actually run these targets, and since they share one output folder
       (src/Directory.Build.props' BaseOutputPath), each ends up copying the same repo-root files
       into that same folder rather than the two colliding on a differently-sourced file.

       Deliberately imperative Copy tasks, not declarative "None Include CopyToOutputDirectory"
       items: an item like that, even declared here rather than directly in Base.csproj, still
       propagates through ProjectReference to whatever references the owning project, and
       Hello.Tests/Service.Tests both reference Hello.csproj/Service.csproj (for InternalsVisibleTo
       access), so it leaked settings.json/settings.local.json into their own test bins too,
       corrupting test isolation the same way putting the items directly in Base.csproj corrupted
       every test project's isolation (see git history). A Copy task tied explicitly to $(OutDir)/
       $(PublishDir) only ever touches the owning project's own output, never a referencing
       project's, since there's no item for MSBuild's cross-project content-propagation machinery to
       discover. -->
  <Target Name="CopySettingsToOutput" AfterTargets="Build" Condition="'$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' OR '$(OutputType)' == 'WinExe'">
    <!-- The real, committed default config, always copied. -->
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\settings.json" DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
    <!-- Personal, gitignored dev override (see .gitignore), convenient to have alongside the app
         in a local `dotnet build` output so running straight from out/ picks it up. Never copied to
         $(PublishDir) (see CopySettingsToPublish below): installer/Setup.wixproj globs its entire
         publish output into the installer, so a developer's own local override must never end up
         there. Skipped entirely when absent, it's optional, a fresh checkout without one
         shouldn't fail the build. -->
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\settings.local.json" DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\settings.local.json')" />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="CopySettingsToPublish" AfterTargets="Publish" Condition="'$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' OR '$(OutputType)' == 'WinExe'">
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\settings.json" DestinationFolder="$(PublishDir)" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

</Project>
